-- Q4010: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A gift from a parent to a child (one generation below)","A bequest from a grandparent directly to a grandchild whose parent is alive","A gift between spouses of the same generation","A transfer to a qualified charitable organization"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 2612(a), the GSTT applies to transfers to skip persons — individuals two or more generations below the transferor per Sec. 2613(a). A grandchild whose parent is still living is a skip person, making the direct gift subject to GSTT.
Wrong (A): A gift from a parent to a child is only one generation apart, so the child is not a skip person.
Wrong (C): Spouses are assigned to the same generation under Sec. 2651(c), so interspousal transfers are never generation-skipping.
Wrong (D): Charitable transfers are excluded from GSTT because charities are not skip persons under the statute.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4010;

-- Q4013: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$200,000, the fair market value at the date of gift","$250,000, the donor''s adjusted basis at the date of gift","$225,000, the average of FMV and the donor''s basis","$0, because gifted property takes a zero basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 1015(a) and Treas. Reg. Sec. 1.1015-1(a)(2), when gifted property has FMV less than the donor''s adjusted basis, the donee uses FMV ($200,000) as the basis for determining a loss. For gain purposes, the donee uses the donor''s basis ($250,000).
Wrong (B): The donor''s adjusted basis applies only for determining gain, not loss, under the dual-basis rule.
Wrong (C): The Code does not average FMV and basis; the dual-basis rule applies distinct values for gain versus loss.
Wrong (D): Gifted property never takes a zero basis; Section 1015 provides either a carryover basis or FMV basis depending on the purpose.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4013;

-- Q4017: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$0, because the annuity returns all contributed value to the grantor","Only the annual gift exclusion amount available per beneficiary","The full $3,000,000 contributed to the trust at inception","The excess appreciation above the Sec. 7520 assumed rate over the term"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): In a zeroed-out GRAT under IRC Sec. 2702, the annuity returns the contributed value plus the Section 7520 assumed return to the grantor. Any appreciation exceeding the 7520 rate (here, 12% actual vs. 5% assumed) passes to remainder beneficiaries transfer-tax-free.
Wrong (A): A zeroed-out GRAT is designed so surplus growth passes to beneficiaries; $0 would only result if actual returns equaled or fell below the 7520 rate.
Wrong (B): The annual gift exclusion is irrelevant to GRAT remainder values; the GRAT gift is measured by the remainder interest at funding.
Wrong (C): The full $3,000,000 cannot pass to beneficiaries because the annuity payments return that principal to the grantor.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4017;

-- Q4019: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["An immediate deduction for the full $80,000 FMV with no capital gains tax","A deduction for the present value of the remainder interest and tax-free sale inside the trust","No charitable deduction until the trust terminates and charity receives assets","A capital loss deduction for the difference between basis and fair market value"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 664, a charitable remainder trust is tax-exempt per Sec. 664(c), so it can sell appreciated property without triggering immediate capital gains tax to the donor. The donor receives a current deduction under Sec. 170(a) equal to the present value of the remainder interest.
Wrong (A): The deduction is limited to the present value of the remainder interest, not the full FMV; the donor retains an income stream that reduces the deductible amount.
Wrong (C): The donor receives the deduction in the year of contribution, not at trust termination.
Wrong (D): No capital loss exists because the property is appreciated (FMV exceeds basis), and the contribution generates a deduction, not a loss.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4019;

-- Q4021: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The present value of the remainder interest must be at least 10% of initial trust FMV","At least 10% of trust income must be distributed to charity each year","The trust must distribute at least 10% of its total assets annually","The charity must receive at least 10% of trust assets upon termination"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 664(d), the present value of the charitable remainder interest must equal at least 10% of the net fair market value of property placed in the trust at contribution. This is calculated using IRS actuarial tables and the Section 7520 rate. Failure means the trust does not qualify as a CRT.
Wrong (B): The 10% test measures the remainder interest value, not annual distributions to charity; CRTs pay income to the non-charitable beneficiary, not to charity, during the trust term.
Wrong (C): CRATs pay a fixed annuity and CRUTs pay a fixed percentage of annually revalued assets, but neither requires distributing 10% of total assets.
Wrong (D): The test is based on the present value of the remainder at funding, not the actual amount the charity receives at termination.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4021;

-- Q4027: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A predeceased parent causes the grandchild to be treated as a non-skip person","The exception applies only to testamentary transfers, not lifetime gifts","The exception eliminates all GSTT on any transfer to a grandchild","The exception applies only when the grandchild is under age 21"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 2651(e), if the transferor''s child (the grandchild''s parent) is deceased at the time of the transfer, the grandchild moves up one generation and is treated as a non-skip person. This eliminates GSTT on the transfer because the grandchild is no longer two generations below the transferor.
Wrong (B): The predeceased parent exception applies to both lifetime gifts and transfers at death; the statute makes no distinction based on timing.
Wrong (C): The exception only reclassifies the grandchild of the deceased parent; it does not eliminate GSTT on transfers to all grandchildren.
Wrong (D): The exception has no age requirement for the grandchild; it depends solely on whether the intervening parent is deceased.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4027;

-- Q4031: Wealth Transfer and Gifting Strategies [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Income and corpus may be spent for the minor before 21, and remaining assets pass to the minor at 21","The trust must distribute all income to the minor on an annual basis","The trust must terminate when the minor reaches age 18 under state law","The minor must serve as the sole trustee of the trust"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 2503(c), a minor''s trust qualifies for the annual exclusion if trust property and income may be expended for the minor before age 21, unexpended amounts pass to the minor at 21, and if the minor dies before 21, assets are payable to the minor''s estate. No Crummey withdrawal right is needed.
Wrong (B): Section 2503(c) requires that income may be distributed, not that it must be distributed annually; the trustee retains discretion to accumulate income.
Wrong (C): The statutory age threshold is 21, not 18; state age-of-majority rules do not control the federal gift tax requirement.
Wrong (D): There is no requirement that the minor serve as trustee; an independent trustee is typical and often advisable.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4031;

-- Q4043: Retirement Plans and Education Savings [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, all traditional IRA contributions are fully deductible regardless of income","No, the deduction is phased out because AGI exceeds the phase-out ceiling","No, the contribution is not deductible at all for any filer","Yes, the contribution is deductible because it is within the $7,500 limit"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 219(g), single taxpayers who are active participants in an employer plan have their traditional IRA deduction phased out between $79,000 and $89,000 MAGI (2026 indexed). At $95,000, the taxpayer exceeds the ceiling, so the deduction is fully eliminated. The $7,500 can still be made as a nondeductible contribution under Sec. 408(o).
Wrong (A): Active participants face income-based phase-out limits; the deduction is not automatic for all contributors.
Wrong (C): The contribution itself is permitted and could be deductible for taxpayers below the phase-out range; the issue here is AGI, not eligibility.
Wrong (D): The $7,500 limit governs the maximum contribution amount, not deductibility; deductibility depends on AGI and plan participation status.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4043;

-- Q4046: Retirement Plans and Education Savings [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The entire $15,000 is tax-free as a qualified distribution","The $10,000 is tax-free and the $5,000 is taxable but penalty-free","The entire $15,000 is taxable as ordinary income to the taxpayer","The $10,000 is tax-free; the $5,000 is taxable plus a 10% early penalty"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 408A(d)(4), Roth IRA contributions are withdrawn first, tax-free and penalty-free. The $10,000 of contributions comes out without tax. The $5,000 of earnings fails the qualified distribution test under Sec. 408A(d)(2) because the five-year holding period is unmet and the taxpayer is under 59½, so earnings are taxable and subject to the 10% penalty under Sec. 72(t).
Wrong (A): The entire amount cannot be tax-free because the five-year holding period requirement has not been satisfied for the earnings portion.
Wrong (B): Earnings from a non-qualified distribution are subject to the 10% early withdrawal penalty, not just income tax.
Wrong (C): Roth IRA contributions are always recovered tax-free because they were made with after-tax dollars; only earnings may be taxable.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4046;

-- Q4050: Retirement Plans and Education Savings [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$70,000 per year for all defined benefit plan participants","$175,000 per year based on years of plan participation","$275,000 per year as the fixed statutory dollar ceiling","The lesser of 100% of high-3 average compensation or $285,000"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 415(b), the maximum annual benefit from a defined benefit plan is the lesser of 100% of the participant''s average compensation for the highest three consecutive years or the annual dollar limit ($285,000 for 2026, indexed for inflation). Benefits before age 62 are actuarially reduced.
Wrong (A): $70,000 is not a statutory limit under Section 415(b); the actual dollar limit is significantly higher.
Wrong (B): $175,000 is not the correct limit, and years of participation affect benefit accrual, not the statutory ceiling itself.
Wrong (C): $275,000 is an outdated figure; the 2026 indexed limit is $285,000, and it must also be compared against the compensation-based limit.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4050;

-- Q4060: Retirement Plans and Education Savings [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$7,500, the full amount of the converted traditional IRA balance","$3,750, representing half of the total conversion amount","The conversion is not permitted under current IRA rules","$0, because the nondeductible basis equals the conversion amount"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): This is the backdoor Roth strategy. The $7,500 contribution was nondeductible (after-tax), and with no earnings or other pre-tax IRA balances, the conversion amount equals the basis, resulting in $0 taxable income. The pro-rata rule under Sec. 408(d)(2) would apply if other pre-tax IRA balances existed.
Wrong (A): The full $7,500 would be taxable only if the contribution had been deductible, creating a pre-tax balance; here the basis offsets the conversion.
Wrong (B): There is no rule halving the taxable amount; the calculation depends on the ratio of pre-tax to after-tax balances.
Wrong (C): Conversions from traditional to Roth IRAs are permitted for all taxpayers regardless of income; income limits on conversions were eliminated in 2010.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4060;

-- Q4083: Entity Tax Compliance: NOLs and Consolidated Returns [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["100% of the current year tax liability only","100% of the prior year tax liability only","The lesser of 100% of current year or 100% of prior year liability","90% of the current year tax liability as the sole safe harbor"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Sec. 6655(d)(1), a non-large corporation avoids the underpayment penalty by paying estimated taxes equal to the lesser of 100% of the current year''s tax liability or 100% of the prior year''s tax liability. Either safe harbor applies to all four installments.
Wrong (A): Paying 100% of current year liability is sufficient but is not the only safe harbor; the prior year alternative also satisfies the requirement.
Wrong (B): Paying 100% of prior year liability also works but is not the exclusive option; the statute provides both alternatives.
Wrong (D): The 90% threshold applies to individual taxpayers under Sec. 6654, not to corporations; the corporate safe harbor requires 100%.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4083;

-- Q4118: International Tax [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Taxed as ordinary income entirely in the current tax year","Allocated ratably over the holding period, taxed at the highest rate plus interest","Taxed as long-term capital gain at the preferential rate","Excluded from income under the participation exemption for foreign dividends"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under the default PFIC rules of Section 1291, excess distributions are allocated ratably over the shareholder''s holding period. Amounts allocated to prior years are taxed at the highest rate in effect for each year, plus an interest charge for deferral. Only the current-year portion is taxed at ordinary rates.
Wrong (A): Only the portion allocated to the current year is taxed as current ordinary income; prior-year allocations bear the highest historical rate plus interest.
Wrong (C): PFIC excess distributions are not eligible for long-term capital gain rates; the punitive regime applies ordinary income rates plus interest.
Wrong (D): The participation exemption under Section 245A applies to dividends from specified 10%-owned foreign corporations, not to PFICs subject to Section 1291.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4118;

-- Q4119: International Tax [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Mark-to-market election under Section 1296","Check-the-box election under Treas. Reg. 301.7701","Section 962 election for individual CFC shareholders","Qualified electing fund election under Section 1295"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): A QEF election under Section 1295 allows the US shareholder to include annually their pro rata share of the PFIC''s ordinary earnings and net capital gain. This current-inclusion regime avoids the punitive interest charge and highest-rate taxation of the default excess distribution method.
Wrong (A): A mark-to-market election under Section 1296 includes unrealized gain or loss annually but does not provide the same ordinary earnings and capital gain pass-through as a QEF.
Wrong (B): A check-the-box election changes entity classification; it does not provide annual income inclusion to avoid PFIC rules.
Wrong (C): A Section 962 election allows individual US shareholders of CFCs to elect corporate tax rates, but it is unrelated to PFIC income inclusion.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4119;

-- Q4132: International Tax [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No tax consequence because intercompany loans are always excluded","A deemed dividend inclusion to the extent of the CFC''s earnings and profits","The CFC recognizes capital gain on the principal amount loaned","The loan is recharacterized as an equity contribution to the parent"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 956, a CFC''s loan to its US parent is treated as an investment in US property. The US shareholder must include in income the lesser of the CFC''s average investment in US property or its accumulated earnings and profits not previously taxed, creating a deemed dividend.
Wrong (A): Intercompany loans are not excluded; Section 956 specifically targets CFC investments in US property, including loans to US shareholders.
Wrong (C): The CFC does not recognize capital gain on a loan; the tax consequence falls on the US shareholder through deemed dividend inclusion.
Wrong (D): The loan is not recharacterized as equity; Section 956 treats it as a deemed repatriation of earnings, not a capital restructuring.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4132;

-- Q4142: International Tax [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Foreign base company sales income under Section 954(d)","Foreign base company services income under Section 954(e)","Foreign personal holding company income under Section 954(c)","Insurance income as defined under Section 953 of the Code"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 954(c), foreign personal holding company income includes dividends, interest, royalties, rents, and annuities. FPHCI is a component of foreign base company income, which is a category of Subpart F income targeting passive investment income earned offshore.
Wrong (A): Foreign base company sales income under Section 954(d) covers income from the sale of property involving a related person, not passive investment income like dividends and interest.
Wrong (B): Foreign base company services income under Section 954(e) involves services performed for or on behalf of a related person, not investment returns.
Wrong (D): Insurance income under Section 953 applies to income attributable to insuring risks outside the CFC''s country of organization, not passive investment earnings.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4142;

-- Q4151: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["A constructive dividend to the shareholder and deemed interest to the corporation","A gift from the corporation to the shareholder under gift tax rules","A capital contribution by the shareholder back to the corporation","A nontaxable return of capital reducing the shareholder''s stock basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Sec. 7872(a), below-market loans between a corporation and shareholder are recharacterized. Forgone interest ($150,000 x 4% = $6,000) is treated as a constructive dividend from corporation to shareholder and a deemed interest payment from shareholder back to the corporation.
Wrong (B): Corporations cannot make gifts; transfers from a corporation to a shareholder are treated as dividends to the extent of E&P, not as gifts.
Wrong (C): The imputed transaction flows from corporation to shareholder, not the reverse; a capital contribution would require the shareholder to transfer value to the corporation.
Wrong (D): A return of capital reduces stock basis, but Sec. 7872 specifically recharacterizes forgone interest as a dividend, not a return of capital.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4151;

-- Q4153: Owner-Entity Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$30,000 recognized as an ordinary loss on the sale","$30,000 recognized as a capital loss on the sale","$0 because the loss is disallowed under Section 267","$15,000 as a limited loss for related party transactions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 267(a)(1), losses on sales between a taxpayer and a corporation in which the taxpayer owns more than 50% are disallowed. Tom owns 60% of Alpha Corp, so the $30,000 loss ($50,000 minus $80,000) is fully disallowed. If Alpha later sells to an unrelated party at a gain, the disallowed loss may offset that gain.
Wrong (A): The loss cannot be recognized as ordinary because Section 267 completely disallows it; the character of the loss is irrelevant when disallowance applies.
Wrong (B): Similarly, the loss cannot be recognized as capital; Section 267 prevents any current recognition regardless of character.
Wrong (D): There is no provision limiting the loss to half; Section 267 disallows the entire loss, not a partial amount.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4153;

-- Q4156: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Sale of stock by a shareholder to an unrelated third-party corporation","Sale of depreciable equipment by a more-than-50% partner to the partnership","Sale of non-depreciable land between related parties at fair market value","Sale of inventory between unrelated parties in an arm''s-length transaction"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Section 1239 recharacterizes gain as ordinary income on sales of depreciable property between related parties. A partner owning more than 50% of a partnership is a related party, and equipment is depreciable by the partnership, triggering ordinary income treatment.
Wrong (A): Stock sales to unrelated corporations do not involve related parties or depreciable property, so Section 1239 does not apply.
Wrong (C): Land is not depreciable property, so even a related-party sale of land falls outside Section 1239''s scope.
Wrong (D): Sales between unrelated parties are arm''s-length transactions and do not trigger Section 1239 regardless of the property type.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4156;

-- Q4162: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No, a profits interest for services is generally nontaxable under Rev. Proc. 93-27","Yes, the partner must recognize $200,000 of ordinary income at receipt","Yes, but the income is treated as capital gain rather than ordinary income","No, all partnership interest receipts are tax-free regardless of type"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Rev. Proc. 93-27, receipt of a profits interest for services is generally not taxable, provided the interest is not disposed of within two years, is not related to a substantially certain income stream, and is not a disguised payment under Sec. 707(a)(2)(A). A profits interest has no liquidation value at grant.
Wrong (B): A profits interest has zero liquidation value at issuance, so there is no amount to include as ordinary income; this treatment applies to capital interests under Sec. 83(a).
Wrong (C): Capital gain treatment does not apply because no taxable event occurs on receipt of a qualifying profits interest.
Wrong (D): Not all partnership interests are tax-free; receipt of a capital interest for services is taxable at FMV under Sec. 83(a) because it conveys rights to existing assets.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4162;

-- Q4163: Owner-Entity Transactions [easy/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Disallowed under Section 267 because Lisa controls both entities","Recognized as an ordinary loss on Lisa''s individual return","Deferred until Beta Corp sells the inventory to a third party","Recognized but limited to $3,000 under the capital loss rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 267(a)(1) disallows losses on sales between a taxpayer and a corporation in which the taxpayer owns more than 50%. Lisa owns 80% of Beta Corp, so the $15,000 loss is fully disallowed. Constructive ownership rules under Section 267(c) can also attribute ownership through family and entities.
Wrong (B): The loss cannot be recognized as ordinary because Section 267 completely disallows it regardless of character.
Wrong (C): Section 267 disallows the loss entirely; it does not defer recognition until a subsequent sale, unlike Section 1091 wash sale rules.
Wrong (D): The $3,000 capital loss limitation under Section 1211(b) applies to net capital losses against ordinary income, not to related-party loss disallowance.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4163;

-- Q4171: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["7.65%, the employee-only share of FICA taxes","12.4% on all amounts with no Medicare component","21%, the flat corporate income tax rate","15.3% on the first $168,600 and 2.9% on the excess"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Sec. 1402(a) and Sec. 707(c), guaranteed payments for services are subject to self-employment tax. The combined rate under Sec. 1401 is 15.3% — 12.4% Social Security tax on earnings up to the wage base ($168,600) and 2.9% Medicare tax with no cap. Above the wage base, only Medicare applies.
Wrong (A): 7.65% is the employee-only portion of FICA; self-employed individuals pay the combined employer and employee share, which totals 15.3%.
Wrong (B): 12.4% is only the Social Security component, and it applies only up to the wage base, not on all amounts; it omits the 2.9% Medicare tax entirely.
Wrong (C): 21% is the corporate income tax rate under Section 11(b), not a self-employment tax rate.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4171;

-- Q4175: Owner-Entity Transactions [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["No effect on basis because only stock basis matters for S corps","Decreases her stock basis by the $200,000 loan amount","Increases her stock basis by the $200,000 loan amount","Creates $200,000 of separate debt basis for additional loss deductions"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1366(d)(1)(B), direct shareholder loans to an S corporation create debt basis that allows loss deductions beyond stock basis. Nancy''s $200,000 loan creates separate debt basis. Losses first reduce stock basis, then debt basis once stock basis reaches zero.
Wrong (A): Both stock basis and debt basis matter; Section 1366(d) explicitly provides for loss deductions against debt basis when stock basis is exhausted.
Wrong (B): A shareholder loan does not decrease stock basis; it creates a separate debt basis category without affecting stock basis.
Wrong (C): The loan creates debt basis, not additional stock basis; these are distinct categories under Section 1367 with different restoration and ordering rules.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4175;

-- Q4178: Owner-Entity Transactions [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["February 28 of the following year, when the partner includes it in income","December 31 of the current year, when the rent accrues on the books","Ratably over the 12 months of the rental period","Never, because the deduction is permanently disallowed for related parties"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Under Section 267(a)(2), when an accrual-method payor and cash-method payee are related parties, the payor cannot deduct the expense until the payee includes it in income. The cash-method partner reports the rent when received on February 28, so the partnership''s deduction is deferred to that year.
Wrong (B): Although the partnership accrues the expense on December 31, Section 267(a)(2) overrides normal accrual timing by matching the deduction to the payee''s inclusion date.
Wrong (C): Ratable allocation over the rental period is not how Section 267(a)(2) works; the deduction is deferred entirely until the payee''s inclusion event.
Wrong (D): The deduction is not permanently disallowed; it is merely deferred until the related payee includes the amount in income.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4178;

-- Q4194: Advanced Basis Calculations [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["The corporation''s total assets on its balance sheet","Cumulative undistributed S income already taxed to shareholders","The corporation''s retained earnings per GAAP financial statements","The total capital contributions made by all shareholders"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Sec. 1368(e)(1), the AAA tracks cumulative S corporation income that has been taxed to shareholders but not yet distributed. AAA determines whether distributions come from previously taxed S income (tax-free) or from accumulated E&P of a prior C corporation period (taxable as dividends under Sec. 1368(c)).
Wrong (A): AAA is a tax-specific account tracking taxed but undistributed income, not a measure of total corporate assets.
Wrong (C): Retained earnings is a GAAP financial reporting concept; AAA is a tax-only construct that may differ significantly from retained earnings.
Wrong (D): Shareholder contributions increase stock basis but are not tracked through the AAA; AAA reflects income items, not capital contributions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4194;

-- Q4217: Entity Formation and Liquidation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, because she transferred property to the corporation","Yes, because combined transferors own 100% of the stock","No, because Janet alone does not meet the 80% control test","No, because Kim contributed only services to the corporation"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 351 requires that transferors of property control the corporation (80% or more) immediately after the exchange. Kim contributed only services, not property, so Kim is excluded from the control group. Janet alone owns 70%, which falls below the 80% threshold, and she must recognize her $100,000 gain.
Wrong (A): Transferring property is necessary but not sufficient; the transferors of property must collectively own at least 80% of the stock after the exchange.
Wrong (B): Combined ownership includes Kim''s shares, but Kim is not a property transferor and cannot be counted toward the 80% control requirement.
Wrong (D): Kim''s services contribution is the reason Kim is excluded from the control group, but the disqualification is Janet''s failure to meet 80% control, not Kim''s contribution type.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4217;

-- Q4231: Entity Formation and Liquidation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Yes, both transferred property and together own at least 80% of stock","Yes, because each transferor individually owns more than 50% of stock","No, because neither transferor individually owns 80% of total stock","No, because boot received by Dave disqualifies the entire transaction"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Section 351 measures control by aggregating all transferors of property. Carol and Dave together own 95% of the stock (50% + 45%), exceeding the 80% threshold. Boot receipt ($5,000 cash to Dave) does not disqualify the transaction; Dave simply recognizes gain to the extent of boot received.
Wrong (B): Section 351 does not require individual 50% ownership; it looks at aggregate control by all property transferors.
Wrong (C): The 80% test is applied to the control group collectively, not to each transferor individually.
Wrong (D): Receipt of boot causes gain recognition to the recipient but does not disqualify the entire Section 351 transaction.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4231;

-- Q4238: Entity Formation and Liquidation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["$10,000 loss on the liquidating distribution","$0, no gain or loss is recognized","$10,000 gain on the liquidating distribution","$30,000 gain equal to the cash received"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under Section 731(a), gain is recognized only if cash received exceeds the partner''s outside basis. Here, $30,000 cash does not exceed the $60,000 outside basis. Loss is recognized in a liquidation only if the partner receives solely cash, unrealized receivables, or inventory — since property was also distributed, no loss is recognized.
Wrong (A): Loss recognition in a liquidating distribution requires receipt of only cash, unrealized receivables, or inventory; the distribution of other property prevents loss recognition.
Wrong (C): Gain arises only when cash exceeds outside basis; $30,000 cash is well below the $60,000 basis, so no gain is triggered.
Wrong (D): The $30,000 cash reduces the partner''s basis but does not create gain because it does not exceed the $60,000 outside basis.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4238;

-- Q4243: Entity Formation and Liquidation [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Ordinary income to the retiring partner","Guaranteed payments under Section 707(c)","Distributions producing capital gain or loss","Self-employment income subject to SE tax"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Section 736(b) payments for a retiring partner''s interest in partnership property (excluding unrealized receivables and unstated goodwill) are treated as distributions under Section 731. This means they result in capital gain if cash exceeds basis, or capital loss in a complete liquidation when only qualifying assets are received.
Wrong (A): Section 736(b) payments are not ordinary income; they are treated as distributions with capital gain or loss character under Section 731.
Wrong (B): Guaranteed payments under Section 707(c) apply to Section 736(a) payments, not to Section 736(b) property payments.
Wrong (D): Section 736(b) distributions are not subject to self-employment tax; only Section 736(a) payments treated as guaranteed payments may be subject to SE tax.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4243;

-- Q4284: Entity Choice and Planning [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #1, #3
UPDATE questions SET
  choices = $EXPL$["Limited liability protection for all partners in the entity","Unlimited number of classes of ownership interests permitted","Lower self-employment taxes compared to S corporation treatment","Ability to make special allocations of income and loss items"]$EXPL$::jsonb,
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Under Section 704(b), partnerships can make special allocations of income, gain, loss, deduction, and credit among partners, provided the allocations have substantial economic effect. S corporations cannot make special allocations and must distribute income pro rata based on stock ownership.
Wrong (A): Limited liability depends on entity form (LP, LLP, LLC); general partners in a general partnership have unlimited liability, so this is not an inherent partnership advantage.
Wrong (B): While partnerships offer flexibility in ownership classes, S corporations are limited to one class of stock; however, partnerships also have structural requirements under the partnership agreement.
Wrong (C): Partnerships generally produce higher SE tax exposure for general partners; S corporation shareholder-employees may reduce SE tax through reasonable salary structuring.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 4284;
